  "final_text": "Both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels render. The rule's status is still \"pending\" because it hasn't run yet.\n\n**Data:** The logs data view (time field `timestamp`) has these main fields:\n- `response`: the HTTP status code, stored as a string.\n- `bytes`: response size.\n- `extension`: the file type requested.\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`: source and destination countries.\n- `clientip`, `host`, `url`, `machine.os` and `agent`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-cb1ca31a Web logs overview\" has five panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Average bytes by extension\n- Top source countries\n\nYou can open it at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-cb1ca31a-web-logs-overview-d15c239a07b3\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-cb1ca31a 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- **Schedule:** it runs every minute.\n- **Condition:** it fires when more than 10 documents match `response.keyword` of `5*` within a 5-minute window.\n- **Rule ID:** `9de634cb-8d5e-4bf8-80d3-74b83dd96218`\n- **No actions:** I didn't attach any, so a firing only shows up as an alert in Kibana. Nothing is sent to Slack, email or any other connector.\n- **Possible no-op:** the sample logs are timestamped when the data was loaded, so a 5-minute window may contain no recent documents, and the rule may never fire.",
